{"edits": [{"finding_ids": ["app/api_errors.py:10:validator"], "search": "# Map Pydantic's error messages to our stable public error codes.
MESSAGE_CODES = {
    "field required": "MISSING",
    "value is not a valid integer": "NOT_AN_INTEGER",
    "ensure this value has at least": "TOO_SHORT",
}", "replace": "# Map Pydantic v2 error types to our stable public error codes.
MESSAGE_CODES = {
    "missing": "MISSING",
    "int_parsing": "NOT_AN_INTEGER",
    "string_too_short": "TOO_SHORT",
}", "explanation": "The failing test shows every error became INVALID. The v1 message strings ("field required", "value is not a valid integer", "ensure this value has at least") no longer match v2 messages ("Field required", "Input should be a valid integer...", "String should have at least 3 characters"), so the prefix lookup misses. In v2, ValidationError.errors() exposes a stable machine-readable 'type' field (missing, int_parsing, string_too_short), so the mapping is keyed on that instead of on localized/changed message text."}, {"finding_ids": ["app/api_errors.py:10:validator"], "search": "            code = next((c for m, c in MESSAGE_CODES.items() if err["msg"].startswith(m)), "INVALID")", "replace": "            code = MESSAGE_CODES.get(err["type"], "INVALID")", "explanation": "Looks up the error code by the v2 error 'type' key rather than by message prefix matching, which was the cause of the INVALID fallback for all errors. Unknown types still fall back to INVALID, preserving the original default."}], "notes": "The previous @validator -> @field_validator + @classmethod migration is correct and is preserved. The test failure was not caused by the validator; it was caused by error_codes() matching v1 message text against v2 error messages, which all fell through to "INVALID". I switched the lookup to the v2 `type` field, which is the stable identifier documented for ValidationError.errors(). Expected results for the test: username missing -> MISSING, age="old" -> NOT_AN_INTEGER. No imports change: Dict, List, BaseModel, Field, ValidationError, field_validator are all still used. The `Field(..., min_length=3)` usage is valid in v2 and was left unchanged. The `next(...)` generator removal is intentional; the dict .get is equivalent for exact-type matching. Behavior note: the v1 prefix matching for TOO_SHORT and similar messages is now exact type matching, which is the intended v2 equivalent."}
